  "scenarios": [
    "Independent flight.launch/block/entity trigger validation and rejection of foreign/unknown trigger shapes before candidate publication.",
    "Actual native snowball, egg, ender pearl, experience bottle, splash potion, lingering potion, trident, normal/tipped/spectral arrow launches and block impacts retain the projectile item identity.",
    "Native offhand throwable and bow inputs retain unrelated main-hand items. Crossbow Multishot launches three custom arrows, preserves each impact identity and consumes one ammunition unit.",
    "Immediate/delayed particle packets use the impact anchor. The native entity collision supplies a living victim; configured damage, cancellation and delayed actions retain native protection.",
    "Projectile payload model changes preserve the remaining inventory metadata/count. Empty payload retirement cancels spawning and does not duplicate native source inventory.",
    "Pre-cancelled launch/hit events, configured launch cancellation and global item permissions preserve native state and skip the corresponding plans.",
    "Launch delays require a live unchanged carrier. Impact receipts survive native removal and hand replacement; reload, foreign same-ID payload changes and unloaded anchors stop stale work without loading the anchor chunk.",
    "A retained native item is recognized at impact after registry reload. Cross-world shooter transfers preserve the impact world and delayed living target binding.",
    "Reload or foreign payload writes from a configured damage callback retire the source before later action steps; already executed preparation effects remain recorded.",
    "Explicit public DamageSource attribution avoids unrelated held melee plans, preserves direct projectile/indirect player causes and native killer credit. Configured explosions retain the projectile entity and honour native explosion protection."
  ],
  "before_fix_reproduction": {
    "jar_sha256": "af0dad743210f419eda86938f34c213ec9b3af82296cf3fccbd8629a55d2f4ae",
    "server": "spigot-1.21.11",
    "java": 21,
    "passed": false,
    "passed_assertions": 4,
    "failed_assertions": 1,
    "summary": "[00:01:57] [Server thread/INFO]: [ProjectileProbe] PROJECTILE_VERIFICATION: FAIL passed=4 failed=1",
    "scope": "An isolated API scenario reproduced a newly held item melee plan firing from configured projectile damage. Four schema assertions passed; the one attribution assertion failed. Development runs are excluded from accepted totals."
  },
  "development_findings": [
    "A fresh owned payload/receipt adapter and Origin.FLIGHT context connect native events to the existing action engine. No reference implementation class was translated.",
    "The first implementation used player-attributed generic damage, which fired a newly held item attack plan. Public DamageSource builders now retain the direct projectile and causing player; explosion sources follow the same ownership.",
    "Impact particles need the retained event anchor instead of the ordinary player-eye origin. Delayed contexts now preserve that origin and compare living targets with the impact world.",
    "Accepted final runs replace preliminary/fixture-development runs; overlapping failed assertions are not separate bug counts."
  ],
  "fixture_corrections": [
    "The 1.21.11 use-item codec requires a rotation vector. The private client now supplies the actual schema; an initial malformed packet/disconnect invalidated that early run.",
    "Cow damage checks compare actual starting health instead of assuming player health. Disposable worlds explicitly select their normal home world and clear residual fixture entities between scenarios.",
    "Official 26.3 particle codec inspection shows the particle payload precedes flags/coordinates. The private bounded observer decodes only the verified no-data FLAME type, rather than assuming an untyped prefix for every particle.",
    "One cold Spigot 26.3 run did not observe the first input. Final fixtures wait for channel registration and settle teleport/inventory updates before sending each input once. Production input/protection timing was not weakened."
